Subject: [E2SM-KPM] key the cumulative-counter tables by UE, not by list position

Three file-scope tables in ran_func_kpm_subs.c hold a UE's PREVIOUS cumulative counter
so the next report can difference it:

    last_pdcp_sdu_total_bytes[]   DRB.PdcpSduVolumeDL / UL
    last_rlc_pdu_total_bytes[]    DRB.UEThpDl / UEThpUl
    last_total_prbs[]             RRU.PrbTotDl / PrbTotUl

All three were indexed by `ue_idx`, which is the UE's position in *this indication's*
list -- ran_func_kpm.c::fill_kpm_ind_msg_frm_3() passes its loop counter -- and not the
UE's identity.  Position is not stable: the matcher rebuilds the list every report, a
subscription carrying a different S-NSSAI condition reports a different subset through
these same tables, and a UE that leaves shifts every UE behind it.  When a slot changes
owner the subtraction becomes one UE's lifetime counter minus another's, so the reported
value is arbitrary.

Measured on a two-cell OAI testbed (2026-09-23, 6000 indications, 38 PRB, band 78):

    cell carrying ONE UE    slot 0 kept its owner for all 1306 samples
                            RRU.PrbTotDl  median      61 [%]      -- correct
                            DRB.UEThpDl   median    8397 [kbps]   -- correct
                                          (~8.4 Mbps of real offered traffic)

    cell carrying TWO UEs   slot 0 changed owner in 2133 of 2188 samples (97%)
    under two S-NSSAI       RRU.PrbTotDl  up to 113020744
    subscriptions           DRB.UEThpDl   median 5534274 (660x too large)

The denominator of RRU.PrbTot* is NOT at fault: node_stats[0] is refreshed once after
the whole UE loop (ran_func_kpm.c:158), so every UE in one indication divides by the same
number, and the single-UE cell runs the same code and stays correct.

This keys the slot on rrc_ue_id.  Both rrc_ue_id and the RNTI identify the UE, but the
RNTI is reassigned on RRC re-establishment while rrc_ue_id is not, and rrc_ue_id is
populated on every path here (the CU-UP path leaves ue_info.ue NULL).

A slot that has just been assigned reports 0 for that one period instead of the UE's
whole lifetime counter, and so does a counter that went backwards -- which is what a
re-established RLC or PDCP entity looks like.  Division by a zero cell aggregate (a
reporting period that carried no traffic) is undefined behaviour and is now guarded.

DRB.RlcSduDelayDl is untouched: it never used ue_idx and reads an average the RLC keeps.
Verified live on both cells -- median 2008.7 ms / 2146.7 ms against an independently
measured 2038 ms application round trip.

NOT APPLIED TO ANY TREE.  This repository never modifies or builds OAI; the patch is
carried as text (see CLAUDE.md).

---
--- a/openair2/E2AP/RAN_FUNCTION/O-RAN/ran_func_kpm_subs.c	2026-09-23 01:16:50.756472649 +0900
+++ b/openair2/E2AP/RAN_FUNCTION/O-RAN/ran_func_kpm_subs.c	2026-09-23 01:16:50.757472671 +0900
@@ -43,6 +43,73 @@
 } uldlcounter_t;
 
 static uldlcounter_t last_pdcp_sdu_total_bytes[MAX_MOBILES_PER_GNB] = {0};
+#if defined (NGRAN_GNB_DU)
+static uldlcounter_t last_rlc_pdu_total_bytes[MAX_MOBILES_PER_GNB] = {0};
+static uldlcounter_t last_total_prbs[MAX_MOBILES_PER_GNB] = {0};
+#endif
+
+/* The three tables above hold a UE's PREVIOUS cumulative counter so the next report can
+   difference it.  They were indexed by ue_idx, which is the UE's position in this
+   indication's list -- fill_kpm_ind_msg_frm_3() passes its loop counter -- and not the
+   UE's identity.  Position is not stable: the matcher rebuilds the list every report, a
+   subscription carrying a different S-NSSAI condition reports a different subset through
+   these same file-scope tables, and a UE that leaves shifts every UE behind it.  When a
+   slot changes owner the subtraction becomes one UE's lifetime counter minus another's,
+   and the reported value is arbitrary.
+
+   Measured on a two-cell testbed, 2026-09-23, 6000 indications:
+
+     cell with one UE   slot 0 kept its owner for all 1306 samples
+                        RRU.PrbTotDl median 61 [%], DRB.UEThpDl median 8397 [kbps]
+                        against ~8.4 Mbps of real traffic -- both correct
+     cell with two UEs  slot 0 changed owner in 2133 of 2188 samples (97%)
+                        RRU.PrbTotDl up to 113020744, DRB.UEThpDl median 5534274
+
+   The denominator is not at fault: node_stats[0] is refreshed once after the whole UE
+   loop, so every UE in one indication divides by the same number, and the single-UE cell
+   runs the same code and stays correct.
+
+   The slot is keyed on rrc_ue_id rather than on the RNTI: both identify the UE, but the
+   RNTI is reassigned on RRC re-establishment while rrc_ue_id is not, and rrc_ue_id is
+   populated on every path here (the CU-UP path leaves ue_info.ue NULL).
+
+   A slot that has just been assigned reports 0 for that one period instead of the UE's
+   whole lifetime counter, and so does a counter that went backwards -- which is what a
+   re-established RLC or PDCP entity looks like. */
+static uint32_t last_counter_ue_id[MAX_MOBILES_PER_GNB] = {0};
+static bool last_counter_taken[MAX_MOBILES_PER_GNB] = {0};
+
+static void clear_counter_slot(const size_t slot)
+{
+  last_pdcp_sdu_total_bytes[slot] = (uldlcounter_t){0};
+#if defined (NGRAN_GNB_DU)
+  last_rlc_pdu_total_bytes[slot] = (uldlcounter_t){0};
+  last_total_prbs[slot] = (uldlcounter_t){0};
+#endif
+}
+
+static size_t counter_slot_for_ue(const uint32_t rrc_ue_id)
+{
+  for (size_t i = 0; i < MAX_MOBILES_PER_GNB; i++)
+    if (last_counter_taken[i] && last_counter_ue_id[i] == rrc_ue_id)
+      return i;
+
+  for (size_t i = 0; i < MAX_MOBILES_PER_GNB; i++)
+    if (!last_counter_taken[i]) {
+      last_counter_taken[i] = true;
+      last_counter_ue_id[i] = rrc_ue_id;
+      clear_counter_slot(i);
+      return i;
+    }
+
+  /* The tables hold MAX_MOBILES_PER_GNB entries and the cell admits at most that many
+     UEs, so this is only reachable if a departed UE's slot was never released.  Reuse
+     slot 0 with a cleared baseline: one zero sample is honest, a cross-UE difference is
+     not. */
+  last_counter_ue_id[0] = rrc_ue_id;
+  clear_counter_slot(0);
+  return 0;
+}
 
